      "title": "Is Bigger Always Better?"
    }
  ],
  "transcript": [
    {
      "start": 42,
      "end": 48,
      "text": "Each neuron multiplies inputs by weights, adds a bias and squashes the result."
    },
    {
      "start": 48,
      "end": 56,
      "text": "Think of a neuron as a tiny voting machine where every input gets a different number of votes."
    },
    {
      "start": 70,
      "end": 78,
      "text": "Weights scale how much each input matters."
    },
    {
      "start": 95,
      "end": 103,
      "text": "Bias shifts the activation threshold."
    },
    {
      "start": 140,
      "end": 146,
      "text": "Non-linear activations let networks model curved decision boundaries."
    },
    {
      "start": 146,
      "end": 154,
      "text": "If you remove the activation, ten layers are mathematically no better than one."
    },
    {
      "start": 175,
      "end": 183,
      "text": "Without them, stacked layers collapse into one linear map."
    },
    {
      "start": 220,
      "end": 226,
      "text": "Depth lets networks build features from simple edges to abstract ideas."
    },
    {
      "start": 226,
      "end": 234,
      "text": "The first layer might see edges, the next sees shapes, and deeper layers see whole objects."
    },
    {
      "start": 255,
      "end": 263,
      "text": "Early layers detect simple patterns, later layers combine them."
    },
    {
      "start": 320,
      "end": 326,
      "text": "Inputs are transformed by each layer until the output layer produces a guess."
    },
    {
      "start": 326,
      "end": 334,
      "text": "The forward pass is just the network making its best guess with the weights it currently has."
    },
    {
      "start": 350,
      "end": 358,
      "text": "Output layer size matches the number of classes."
    },
    {
      "start": 365,
      "end": 371,
      "text": "Training means making this number smaller on average."
    },
    {
      "start": 371,
      "end": 379,
      "text": "Everything in training exists to push this single number down."
    },
    {
      "start": 400,
      "end": 408,
      "text": "Cross-entropy for classification."
    },
    {
      "start": 425,
      "end": 433,
      "text": "Mean squared error for regression."
    },
    {
      "start": 510,
      "end": 516,
      "text": "Stepping against the gradient reduces the loss fastest locally."
    },
    {
      "start": 516,
      "end": 524,
      "text": "This is honestly one of the most beautiful ideas in all of machine learning!"
    },
    {
      "start": 545,
      "end": 553,
      "text": "Imagine walking downhill in fog, feeling the slope under your feet."
    },
    {
      "start": 580,
      "end": 586,
      "text": "The single most important hyper-parameter to tune."
    },
    {
      "start": 586,
      "end": 594,
      "text": "Be careful: a learning rate that is too high can make the loss explode."
    },
    {
      "start": 605,
      "end": 613,
      "text": "Too large overshoots the minimum."
    },
    {
      "start": 620,
      "end": 628,
      "text": "Too small crawls and wastes compute."
    },
    {
      "start": 660,
      "end": 666,
      "text": "Noisy but cheap gradient estimates that also help generalization."
    },
    {
      "start": 666,
      "end": 674,
      "text": "Instead of looking at all million examples, we look at a batch of sixty-four."
    },
    {
      "start": 690,
      "end": 698,
      "text": "One pass over the whole dataset is called an epoch."
    },
    {
      "start": 745,
      "end": 751,
      "text": "Gradients are computed layer by layer, reusing intermediate results."
    },
    {
      "start": 751,
      "end": 759,
      "text": "Backprop is just the chain rule from calculus, applied very systematically."
    },
    {
      "start": 800,
      "end": 808,
      "text": "Automatic differentiation libraries do this for you."
    },
    {
      "start": 850,
      "end": 856,
      "text": "Early layers barely learn when gradients get multiplied by small numbers."
    },
    {
      "start": 856,
      "end": 864,
      "text": "This problem stalled deep learning for years, which is a real limitation of naive deep networks."
    },
    {
      "start": 890,
      "end": 898,
      "text": "ReLU activations help gradients flow."
    },
    {
      "start": 905,
      "end": 913,
      "text": "Residual connections add shortcuts for the signal."
    },
    {
      "start": 980,
      "end": 986,
      "text": "Training loss keeps falling while validation loss rises."
    },
    {
      "start": 986,
      "end": 994,
      "text": "Watch out: a perfect score on training data is a warning sign, not a victory."
    },
    {
      "start": 1010,
      "end": 1018,
      "text": "More data is the most reliable cure."
    },
    {
      "start": 1050,
      "end": 1056,
      "text": "Forces the network to spread knowledge across many units."
    },
    {
      "start": 1056,
      "end": 1064,
      "text": "Dropout is like making a team practice with random members missing."
    },
    {
      "start": 1080,
      "end": 1088,
      "text": "Disabled at inference time."
    },
    {
      "start": 1120,
      "end": 1126,
      "text": "Held-out data used to pick hyper-parameters and stop training early."
    },
    {
      "start": 1126,
      "end": 1134,
      "text": "The validation curve tells you when to stop."
    },
    {
      "start": 1150,
      "end": 1158,
      "text": "Never tune on the test set."
    },
    {
      "start": 1230,
      "end": 1236,
      "text": "Bigger models keep improving, but at rising cost — critics argue for efficiency."
    },
    {
      "start": 1236,
      "end": 1244,
      "text": "This is a genuinely controversial topic and people disagree strongly."
    },
    {
      "start": 1275,
      "end": 1283,
      "text": "Compute and energy costs grow quickly."
    },
    {
      "start": 1330,
      "end": 1336,
      "text": "Tools like saliency maps offer partial, sometimes misleading explanations."
    },
    {
      "start": 1336,
      "end": 1344,
      "text": "We can measure what the network does far better than we can explain why."
    },
    {
      "start": 1370,
      "end": 1378,
      "text": "Important for medicine, finance and law."
    }
  ]
}